@flexiarg operator/resume-the-main-thread-once-the-detour-closes
@title Resume the Main Thread Once the Detour Closes
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 2 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-XSSh3n turn-Zfslji; first proposed for: A side-quest (here: the warrant infrastructure) had displaced the main line of work (War Machine PROOF-2a), and the operator judges the side-quest finished.
@why [vsatelier/return-loop]

! conclusion: Name the resumption explicitly ('we can now get back to X'), tie it to the detour's completion with 'now', and hedge the completeness honestly rather than demanding or faking a clean finish.

  + IF:
    The detour is done enough that the precondition it represented no longer blocks, even if not perfectly ('or just about').

  + HOWEVER:
    Resuming too early reopens the work on a shaky foundation; waiting for perfect closure lets a finished-enough detour keep blocking real work.

  + THEN:
    Name the resumption explicitly ('we can now get back to X'), tie it to the detour's completion with 'now', and hedge the completeness honestly rather than demanding or faking a clean finish.

  + BECAUSE:
    Return-loop feeds an episode's outcomes back as the start of the next story; this specialises it to the operator's scheduling case — the warrant episode's outcome (a ready tool) is exactly what reopens the suspended PROOF-2a thread.
